<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            The node-graph framework the shader, VFX and animation graphs are all built on: the
            document model with its ports, edges, groups and comments; the generated registry that
            turns [Node]-attributed C# classes into a node library; and the compiler that walks a
            graph in dependency order, resolves dynamically typed ports, and reports every
            complaint against the node and port it came from.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>editor;node-graph;shader-graph;vfx;source-generator;game-engine;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core\Vixen.Core.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Mathematics\Vixen.Core.Mathematics.csproj" />

        <!--
          The undo stack an edit is recorded on. Doc 11 puts the view in this assembly beside the
          model, and a view whose gestures did not produce IEditorCommands would be a second way to
          change a graph — which is the one thing Vixen.Editor.Core exists to prevent. The same
          argument Vixen.Editor.Inspector's csproj makes, and the same reference.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.Core\Vixen.Editor.Core.csproj" />

        <!--
          The rows a port's value is edited in. `NodePortEditProvider` describes a node's ports as
          `InspectorMember`s, which is what puts them in the ordinary inspector panel instead of the
          hand-written one `NodeInspector` used to be — see doc 36 § P1.

          ⚠ The direction is the one the other toolsets already take: Terrain and Water both
          reference this assembly, and Vixen.Editor.Inspector references neither of them. The
          narrow `IEditMember` contract would have avoided the reference and would also have avoided
          getting a port a drawer, a reset button or a row, which is the whole point.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.Inspector\Vixen.Editor.Inspector.csproj" />

        <!--
          NodeCanvas, NodeMinimap and the wire layer: doc 09 built them for exactly this consumer.
          Vixen.Ui and Vixen.Ui.Controls arrive with it.

          ⚠ This is what makes the assembly a UI assembly, and it is worth being awake to: anything
          that only wants to *compile* a graph — a build task, an importer — now links the interface
          framework through it. Splitting the view out would fix that and would split the model from
          the only thing that edits it; doc 11 names one assembly, and this is it.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced\Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced.csproj" />

        <!-- The descriptors the generator writes are TypeDescriptors, so the assembly needs the type. -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Reflection\Vixen.Core.Reflection.csproj" />

        <!--
          The document types are [DataContract]s of this assembly's own, so it runs the descriptor
          generator over itself — analyzers do not flow through a ProjectReference. Note what is
          *not* here: Vixen.Core.Yaml. Which file format a graph is written in belongs to whoever
          writes it, so anything that only wants to compile a graph links no parser.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Reflection.Generator\Vixen.Core.Reflection.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />

        <!--
          And the generator that reads [Node] classes. Same arrangement, and the reason a node
          library is a folder of classes rather than a table somebody has to remember to update.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.Generator\Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Editor.NodeGraph.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
        ⚠ **Imported for the `**/*.vcss` glob, which is what makes `NodeGraphTheme.vcss` an
        EmbeddedResource.** `NodeGraphTheme.Css` reads it back by its logical name, so without this
        line the property throws at the first `Install` rather than failing to compile — the error
        message it throws names this import for that reason.

        At the bottom of the file, because the glob has to run after DefaultItemExcludes is final.
        There is no `.vxml` in this project, so the other half of the .targets is inert here.
    -->
    <Import Project="..\..\Core\Vixen.Ui\build\Vixen.Ui.targets" />

</Project>
